  "body": "A rival way of posing the problem: not 'how do we make the flat's agreements stick' but 'what can the asker do, starting now and possibly alone, on the assumption that the agreements may never stick.' The shift matters because every remedy in the selected account — the shared note, the confirmation lines, the review conversation — still draws on the scarcest resource in the flat, which is the avoider's participation and, more generally, all three people's appetite for joint administration. I want a plan that works even if the talks never improve.\n\nThe diagnosis I would build on is different too. The chores in this flat live entirely inside a negotiation economy: nothing is owned, nothing is priced, every allocation is temporary and must have its meaning re-established each time. In such a system, disagreeing later about what was agreed is not a malfunction to be engineered away; it is the steady state, because 'what counts as done' and 'what is fair given the rota' are genuinely contestable, and each added definition just produces more text to quarrel over. On this reading, writing agreements down does not remove the friction — it upgrades the litigation. A shared note among friends is also a record, and arguing from a record moves three friends closer to three counterparties. Worse, if the selected account's own unresolved fork is real and the ambiguity is masking a standing fairness grievance, a document can freeze the unfair split in place and hand the wronged person something new to swallow.\n\nSo the rival prescription is to shrink the domain that requires agreement, in four moves ordered by how much buy-in they need. First, partition rather than rotate: claim whole domains outright and permanently — I do the kitchen, full stop; the bathroom is yours if you will take it. Ownership dissolves both recurring arguments at once, since 'whose turn' disappears and 'what done means' becomes the owner's standard, with the corollary that non-owners forfeit commentary. The asker can begin this unilaterally: announce a domain, carry it on a fixed cadence regardless of how the talks go, and let visible reliability be the argument. Second, convert what can be converted into money: a cleaner every two or three weeks, split three ways, removes a large fraction of the contested commons in one stroke, and money is the one chore currency adults exchange without imputations about character. Third, where a deal is unavoidable, make it too small to fight about — 'I have the bins this week' rather than a standing arrangement — so that being wrong about it costs days, not months of accumulated grievance. This is the opposite instinct from defining 'done' at greater length: it treats ambiguity as permanent and prices it instead of fighting it.\n\nFourth, re-read the avoider before approaching them. The selected account reads avoidance as a rational retreat from aversive talks and proposes a one-to-one about making the talks bearable; but that conversation still presupposes the talks are mandatory. A rival reading: the avoidance is a revealed preference for less joint management — the friendship matters to this person, the chore polity does not. The test and the remedy are the same move: offer exits instead of meetings. Take the bins permanently and skip the discussions; or chip in a bit more and we hire help. If they take an exit with relief, the avoidance was never about the tone of the talks — it was about being governed at all. If no exit attracts them either, then the avoidance is about something under the chores, and the honest conversation is 'what do you want this flat to be,' which no chore architecture can pre-script for them.\n\nOne thing the original question should not lose: the failure mode here is not a dirty kitchen. It is three friends becoming three litigants. Whatever of this the asker adopts, the friendship needs protecting on purpose — time together that is not flat administration — because the most worrying detail in the description is that the arguments may be the friendship now.\n\nWhat I cannot resolve: partition needs each person willing to own a domain at a standard the others can live with, and if the avoider owns something and does it badly, the conflict returns as standards-policing, uglier than the turn-taking rows it replaced. The money move assumes slack that may not exist. At those failure points the selected account's conversation-first route stops being a rival and becomes the necessary one.",
